ROADMAP.md:341:5. whether capability and type arguments are inferred or written (decided: capability arguments are written, a revision of OD45 and OD87);
ROADMAP.md:409:| R-0566 | One live home for binding decisions (decision row P20, approved 2026-11-05; the decisions are [recorded](docs/decisions.md#decided-project) in `docs/decisions.md`, which the move places at `docs/decisions.md`) | the still-binding architecture and process decisions of the archived roadmap log (new compiler and retention contract, authority, allocation, Lean as the specification, the owner-recording rule) are in `docs/decisions.md` with their dates and revision links; the owner-approved revisions (OD45/OD87, TYPE-22, TRAIT-1 and any other accepted row) are recorded there; ROADMAP.md, PHILOSOPHY.md and AGENTS.md cite it and restate nothing; a gate refuses a roadmap rule link into the archived log once the move lands | — |
ROADMAP.md:463:| R-0585 | Vacuity checks and contract-strength labels from the first proof slice | no fact reads "proved" when its theorem holds only under an unsatisfiable precondition or premise set, from the first proof slice on; the checker reports a context as infeasible wherever its octagon is empty, and a discharge inside an infeasible context is marked vacuous, never established, unless the infeasibility itself is the claim (a dead branch), applying OD120 from B1 rather than F; each proved claim carries its strength (a point instance, one direction, an equivalence or a full contract), so a single-input proof never reads as a universal contract; planted vacuous and point-only proofs are caught; obligation sufficiency stays with R-0460 and the phase-F validation project with R-0219 | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:465:| R-0600 | A derived "always aborts" fact (SPARK `No_Return`) | a function whose every path aborts gets a derived fact, with no annotation and no function category (OD116); the facts report shows it, and the checker uses it to prune paths after a call to an abort wrapper within the decided divergence typing (TYPE-4@2); any change to the join rule goes to the owner; due with the functions-and-calls slice | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:469:| R-0577 | Reproducible builds checked in CI from slice 0 ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-reproducible-builds), 2026-11-06, revised 2026-11-07) | the same source, compiler revision, toolchain pin and target give byte-identical BIR, LLVM IR text, LCB job and output, and final binary; pins cover the nix flake, LCB revision and LLVM/clang version; artifacts contain no timestamps, absolute paths or host names (`-ffile-prefix-map`, `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, deterministic link order), and output never depends on hash-map iteration order; CI builds every slice-0 example twice in different directories and on a GitHub runner and concrete-ci-2 and compares SHA-256, failing on any difference; `concrete facts` carries the compiler SHA, toolchain pins and target; work started on `wip/slice0-repro`; Home: tooling (build pins and CI). Gate: reproducibility, every slice-0 example built in two directories and on two machines (a GitHub runner and concrete-ci-2), SHA-256 compared ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0503 |
ROADMAP.md:478:| R-0561 | Bug ledger by compiler | legacy defects are marked retired with the old implementation, never fixed, each keeping its reproducer, ruling and structural class; std defects move with std as contracts and regressions; every legacy ruling that states language behaviour becomes a conformance example or a recorded owner question; the minimal issue record of the day-one root becomes the new ledger with the first slice | R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:522:| R-0604 | Audit the RocksDB and ethlambda acceptance programs before accepting them (owner, 2026-11-06; revised 2026-11-07) | each program's needs are listed against the decided language; concurrency is now final slice 28, with its model still to be decided, and ethlambda follows it ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-final-slices)); foreign function values (OD4) stay refused, and any real need for them goes to the owner before acceptance; Home: tooling (the audit record). Gate: each program's needs listed against the decided language before acceptance ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | — |
ROADMAP.md:538:| R-0524 | Frames and entry-state reasoning without `old` (OD117) | frames are proved on the model; a postcondition reaches the entry state only through an explicit Lean theorem; no source `old` or snapshot construct exists | R-0473 |
ROADMAP.md:539:| R-0526 | Total spec functions (OD115) and per-function termination proofs, with no total-function category (OD116) | every recursive edge of a contract-referenced spec function is well-founded or refused; an ordinary function's termination proof is a theorem about that function | R-0496 |
ROADMAP.md:576:| R-0568 | Day-one root infrastructure (T0) | on main beside the old tree: the new root's CI jobs; `required-checks.txt` enforced by `train.sh watch` and `land`; a required-check transition mechanism (an explicit, validated old-to-new job mapping; replacement checks must pass at the exact candidate SHA; a control that dropping a requirement without a mapping is refused; no legacy check is ever marked passed); `ISSUES.tsv` (ID, evidence, owner, severity, closure condition) with its validator and controls; the archive records of the stopped services | R-0562, R-0564 |
ROADMAP.md:577:| R-0558 | Move the old tree to `legacy/` in one train and set up the minimal root. Day one, the root holds the README, this file, AGENTS.md, an empty CHANGELOG, LICENSE, the new build files, `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean`, `docs/PHILOSOPHY.md`, `docs/decisions.md`, the landing, review and hook scripts, `scripts/oracle/`, `required-checks.txt` and its job mapping, `RETENTION.tsv`, the minimal issue record `ISSUES.tsv` with its validator, and the [2026-11 roadmap archive](docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/README.md) with its task disposition table. Everything else comes back from `legacy/` when a slice needs it. The status page, the work-queue dispatcher, the fuzz farm, perf tracking and the nightly campaign stop, and each is listed in `RETENTION.tsv` as archived; each returns only with a roadmap task. The ci-3 fuzz corpus, perf history and leanprof data were not copied before the handover (decision row P14) and are not archived. | the root holds only that set; a root check refuses any modification or addition under `legacy/`, and any deletion its `RETENTION.tsv` row does not allow (controls for each); no legacy gate runs; the move lands on both remotes | T0, R-0563, R-0565, owner: approval of the move inventory |
ROADMAP.md:579:| R-0563 | Operational migration acceptance test (T1), before the move | T0 is on main; on a throwaway branch holding the exact proposed day-one root, a fresh clone builds the candidate (after `train.sh build`, so the candidate tree is what builds) with the candidate's own landing tooling, hooks, runs exact-SHA CI and passes a dry-run landing; negative controls (a broken build, a failing hook gate, a failed required CI job, a missing, skipped or cancelled required check, a SHA mismatch) are each refused for the expected reason at the expected SHA; a check that cannot run on a fallback runner is reported incomplete and blocks landing, never passed; the two-remote landing is proved by the move itself | R-0568, owner: approval of the move inventory |
ROADMAP.md:580:| R-0564 | A pinned, reproducible legacy oracle, independent of `legacy/` | `legacy-compiler-final` builds in an isolated checkout of the tag from a pinned toolchain (`scripts/oracle/ORACLE.lock`) into a recorded digest; the build is published as a release artifact whose identity and SHA-256 are in the lock, fetched and verified by digest on any runner, cached on concrete-ci-2 and rebuildable from the tag alone with a hash check; a missing or mismatched artifact makes the oracle check incomplete, which blocks landing; runner criteria (decision row P13, owner 2026-11-05: concrete-ci-2 and GitHub-hosted runners as a regular second pool): the lock records the release tarball's SHA-256 with the toolchain and flake-lock hashes; landing and oracle runs use separate pools with per-pool CPU and memory limits and at most two heavy runs per machine; jobs that need ci-2's caches run there, and the build, the spec, the gates and the oracle diff also run on the GitHub-hosted x86_64-linux pool, which takes them over when ci-2 is down; a check neither pool can run is incomplete and blocks landing, and a heavy campaign the GitHub pool cannot run is recorded as skipped, never passed; self-hosted runners never run pull-request jobs; a weekly from-scratch rebuild matches the digest; the differential harness invokes it by digest when the new compiler changes; an unexplained difference fails, and the oracle's agreement never overrides the Lean semantics | — |
