docs/decisions.md:136:  growth, reserve and multi-allocation transition contracts; applicability of container `Destroy`
docs/decisions.md:148:  and foreign-mutation taxonomy and call-only annotation syntax, undecided on 2026-11-03, are provisionally decided by rows Q3.7–Q3.12 and Q9.7 ([rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); OD64's confinement
docs/decisions.md:185:- **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
docs/decisions.md:186:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
docs/decisions.md:187:- **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
docs/decisions.md:190:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
docs/decisions.md:191:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
docs/decisions.md:218:- **Q2.8** How is 'Copy or Destroy' written for container impls? — (a) separate impls for every admitted combination of Copy/Destroy payload bounds, no disjunctive-bound syntax: two each for Heap, HeapArray and Vec, three for Result, four for OrderedMap; coherence may use Copy/Destroy disjointness. *Revises or adds: TRAIT-1@1 (under OD6): equal generic impl heads allowed only when Copy/Destroy disjointness makes their domains disjoint; all other coherence and orphan rules unchanged. Option (b) would also add new bound syntax.*
docs/decisions.md:237:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
docs/decisions.md:238:- **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
docs/decisions.md:241:- **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
docs/decisions.md:295:- **P7** Prove the new landing path before moving — T0: land beside the old tree the new root CI, required-checks.txt (enforced by train watch and land), RETENTION.tsv, the task disposition table, ORACLE.lock with the oracle artifact, and ISSUES.tsv. T1: rehearse the move on a throwaway branch: fresh clone, build, hook, exact-SHA CI, train --dry-run land; a broken build, a failing gate and a missing required check must each be refused. T2: the move in one train.
docs/decisions.md:297:- **P9** Website during the transition — The website goes down at the move and is redone from scratch with the new compiler; no frozen playground is served. Unpublishing the live site is an outward action whose moment the owner confirms.
docs/decisions.md:298:- **P10** What 'closed' means for old-compiler bugs — Old-compiler defects become 'retired with the old implementation', never 'fixed'. Each keeps its reproducer, ruling and structural class; 106 reproducers feed the oracle comparisons. Bugs the frozen browser compiler still shows stay visible while the site serves it.
docs/decisions.md:304:- **P16** Status changes in the refusal list — Accept the new statuses in PHILOSOPHY.md. Now Permanent: operator overloading (OD26), capability hiding (authority rule 5), derived equality, mutable global state (OD8), umbrella capabilities and aliases, old/quantifiers in contracts (OD117), lexical shadowing (OD67), and ghost code that affects execution. Ghost code itself is admitted as decided (OD81). Foreign function values reopen when typed extern fn types are admitted (OD4); concurrency reopens on R-0399's trigger.
docs/decisions.md:307:- **P18** Phase E: contracts and termination within OD115–OD117 — Phase E: no source old, quantifier, implication or snapshot construct (OD117); entry/exit and frame properties are Lean theorems (a source frame clause would be a new decision for you); spec functions have a body and are total (OD115); ordinary functions have no total category (OD116) and get per-function termination theorems where wanted. R-0524 becomes 'Frame and entry/exit theorems in Lean, no old'; R-0526 stays 'checked totality of spec functions'.
docs/decisions.md:310:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
docs/decisions.md:312:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
